# LibreWolf update-check.patch  (Android)
# Owner: LW-M6-06 ("Implement in-app update checking without an app store")
#
# Upstream files touched
# -----------------------
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/build.gradle                                   (2 buildConfigFields)
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/java/org/mozilla/fenix/HomeActivity.kt (1 call in onResume)
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/java/org/mozilla/fenix/settings/SettingsFragment.kt (row visibility)
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/res/xml/preferences.xml                (the switch row)
# New files
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/java/org/mozilla/fenix/lw/UpdateCheck.kt
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/res/values/lw_update_check.xml
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/test/java/org/mozilla/fenix/lw/UpdateCheckerTest.kt
#
# Line numbers are from the ESR tree (firefox-153.0esr, ./version.android), AFTER
# the patches listed before this one in assets/patches/android.txt.
#
#
# What it is
# -----------
# The direct-APK distribution has no store to tell its users an update exists.
# docs/android/DISTRIBUTION.md is the contract; this is the code. In one
# paragraph: a switch in Settings (About category), OFF by default, with a
# summary that says exactly what is sent and where. When it is on, and only
# then, HomeActivity.onResume asks UpdateCheck.maybeRun, which at most once per
# 24 h fetches two static files from the distribution host -- latest.json and
# latest.json.sig -- verifies the ECDSA P-256 / SHA-256 signature against a
# public key embedded at build time, and if the signed document names a newer
# version than the running one, shows a dialog with an "Open download page"
# button. It never downloads, never installs, never retries in a loop, and
# treats every failure (network, parse, signature) as "no update".
#
#
# The privacy properties, and where each one is pinned
# -----------------------------------------------------
#   * Nothing identifies the install. The request carries a static User-Agent
#     ("Redoubt-UpdateCheck/1", the same literal on every install), no cookies
#     (CookiePolicy.OMIT), no cache validators (useCaches=false, so no ETag or
#     If-Modified-Since that could act as a per-install token), follows no
#     redirects, and is a private load. It sends NOT EVEN the version string:
#     the comparison happens on the device, so the server learns only that
#     some Redoubt asked. UpdateCheckerTest pins every one of those request
#     fields, because a packet capture cannot see inside TLS.
#   * Off by default, opt-in, disclosed. android:defaultValue="false"; the
#     summary names the host, the cadence, what is and is not sent.
#   * Foreground only, rate-limited. The only caller is onResume; there is no
#     WorkManager job, timer or service. UpdateCheck.MIN_INTERVAL_MS = 24 h,
#     tracked in its own SharedPreferences file (lw_update_check).
#   * Store builds do not double-notify, by ARTIFACT not by runtime query.
#     build.gradle reads -PlwUpdateCheckPubkey; without it
#     BuildConfig.LW_UPDATE_CHECK_PUBKEY is "", UpdateCheck.isCompiledIn is
#     false, SettingsFragment hides the row, and maybeRun returns on its first
#     line. The F-Droid and Accrescent pipelines simply do not pass the key.
#     scripts/android-apk.sh forwards LW_UPDATE_CHECK_PUBKEY (and, for the
#     smoke harness, LW_UPDATE_CHECK_ENDPOINT) from the environment.
#   * The private key never enters the build or this repository. What the
#     build embeds is the base64 DER SubjectPublicKeyInfo. Generating and
#     holding the key is LW-M6-01's custody problem, not this patch's.
#
#
# Why a detached .sig and not a "signature" field inside the JSON
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# DISTRIBUTION.md's first draft put the signature inside the document, "over
# this document". A signature over a JSON object needs a canonical form (which
# whitespace, which key order, how numbers are written) agreed between the
# maintainer's signing script and the Kotlin verifier, and a mismatch there is
# a silent "no update" forever. Signing the exact served bytes and shipping the
# signature next to them (the minisign/signify shape) has no canonical form to
# get wrong: `openssl dgst -sha256 -sign key.pem latest.json | base64 -w0 >
# latest.json.sig`. DISTRIBUTION.md is updated to match.
#
#
# Why ECDSA P-256 and not Ed25519
# --------------------------------
# java.security.Signature "Ed25519" exists only from API 33; SHA256withECDSA
# and the EC KeyFactory are in every Android release Fenix supports, and
# openssl produces and verifies the same signatures on the maintainer's side.
#
#
# What this patch does NOT do, on purpose
# ----------------------------------------
#   * No endpoint exists yet: redoubtbrowser.org is unregistered (IDENTITY.md).
#     A build made without a key has no reachable code; a build made with a
#     key against a host that does not resolve gets NoResult and stays silent.
#   * No key is checked in. The release pipeline passes the PUBLIC key when the
#     maintainer has one.
#   * No notification-channel plumbing: the prompt is an in-app dialog on the
#     home activity, which needs no permission on any API level.
#
#
# Ordering
# ---------
# Shares fenix app/build.gradle with no-adjust, no-glean, no-gms,
# no-crashreporter, fenix-abi-split and branding (an ORDERED chain);
# HomeActivity.kt with no-nimbus, no-adjust and no-suggest. preferences.xml,
# SettingsFragment.kt and the three new files are shared with nothing. Every
# pair was replayed from the pristine ESR copy in list order and with this
# patch moved in front of each partner; results byte-identical, exit 0, no .rej
# -- see scripts/check-patch-order.py's REVIEWED_ORDER_FREE. Listed last in
# assets/patches/android.txt.
#
#
# Verification
# -------------
#   ./mach gradle fenix:testDebugUnitTest  -> UpdateCheckerTest (8 tests)
#   ./scripts/android-smoke.sh --check-update-privacy --serial <dev> --apk <apk>
#     with the switch OFF: no traffic to the update host across launch and the
#     settings screens; with it ON (flipped through the UI): exactly one host
#     is contacted, the update host, and nothing else new. Store-style builds
#     (no key) pass because the row does not exist and nothing is sent.
#   Evidence: docs/android/evidence/lw-m6-06/.
